The '''2022 Thurrock Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022 to elect a third of the members of [[Thurrock Council]] in England.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Election dates {{!}} Elections and election results |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/elections-and-election-results/election-dates |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref> Sixteen of the council's 49 seats were contested in sixteen of [[Thurrock]]'s twenty [[electoral wards]].<ref name=":results" /><ref name=":wardchanges" /> There are two or three seats in each ward depending on its population.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Wards {{!}} Wards and polling stations |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/wards-and-polling-districts/wards |access-date=2022-06-11 |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref> This was on the same day as other [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|local elections]] across the [[United Kingdom]].

The [[Conservatives (UK)|Conservatives]] won the popular vote by 91 votes and retained their thirty-seat majority in the council while [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] came out with fourteen seats, the lowest amount they have ever held on the council.<ref name=":ElectionOverview" /> The average turnout for the election was 26.41% per ward, meaning that one in four of all of Thurrock's eligible voters had participated.<ref name=":ElectionOverview" />

== Election background ==
In the run-up to the election, both the [[Conservatives (UK)|Conservatives]] and [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] ran on local manifestos focusing on financial prosperity. The Conservatives, who administered the council, promised continued local investment and aid to combat the [[Cost of living crisis (UK)|cost of living crisis]],<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2022-04-21 |title=Thurrock Tories hope to focus on local economy during elections |url=https://www.yourthurrock.com/2022/04/21/thurrock-tories-hope-to-focus-on-local-economy-during-elections/ |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=Your Thurrock |language=en-US}}</ref> while Labour, the council's main opposition, criticised the Conservatives for high [[Council Tax|council tax]], overborrowing, overspending and an alleged lack of transparency, promising to instead focus on openness and the community.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2022-04-21 |title=Thurrock Labour unveil election manifesto |url=https://www.yourthurrock.com/2022/04/21/thurrock-labour-unveil-election-manifesto/ |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=Your Thurrock |language=en-US}}</ref> There were also accusations by the Conservative council leader Rob Gledhill that Labour's councillors had supported the [[Just Stop Oil]] protests in the borough, adding that the Labour Group on the council had "close links" to the organisation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Speight |first=Neil |date=2022-04-25 |title=Political war of words continues as Tory councillors step up level of accusations |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/political-war-of-words-continues-as-tory-councillors-step-up-level-of-accusations-130343 |access-date=2022-06-12 |website=Thurrock Nub News |language=en}}</ref>

The Conservatives won and lost two seats, retaining their thirty-seat majority in the council.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-05-06 |title=Local Elections 2022: Results in Kent, Sussex and Essex |url=https://www.itv.com/news/meridian/2022-05-06/local-elections-2022-results-in-kent-sussex-and-essex |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=ITV News |language=en}}</ref> One Conservative to lose his seat was councillor [[David Van Day]], who lost to Labour's Srikanth Panjala by twenty votes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lee |first=Joseph |date=2022-05-06 |title=Elections 2022: Magic pixies and an '80s pop star - five under-the-radar moments |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-61346824 |access-date=2022-05-06}}</ref><ref name=":results">{{Cite web |title=Thurrock Council - Declaration of result of poll for local elections, 5 May 2022 |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/sites/default/files/assets/documents/20220505-lg-result-v01.pdf |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref> Labour won two seats in total but lost another three, resulting in a fourteen-seat minority; the lowest amount of seats Labour has ever held on the council.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2022-05-06 |title=Thurrock Conservatives retain control of council |url=https://www.yourthurrock.com/2022/05/06/thurrock-conservatives-retain-control-of-council/ |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=Your Thurrock |language=en}}</ref> The [[Thurrock Independents]] stood its leader's son Ross Byrne in the ward of Stanford East & Corringham Town,<ref name=":ElectionOverview" /> although their own seats were uncontested. A non-aligned [[independent councillor]] was elected in the Tilbury Riverside & Thurrock Park ward, leaving a total of two independent councillors in the council.<ref name=":results" />

Mayor Sue Shinnick<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Mayor {{!}} Mayor of Thurrock |url=https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/mayor-of-thurrock/mayor |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513145839/https://www.thurrock.gov.uk/mayor-of-thurrock/mayor |archive-date=2022-05-13 |access-date=2022-05-08 |website=Thurrock Council}}</ref> vacated her Labour seat in Ockendon to stand in the Conservative-held South Chafford ward. Although she won, her old seat fell to Conservative councillor Paul Arnold. Labour's loss was attributed to changing demographics in the [[South Ockendon]] area.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Speight |first=Neil |date=2022-05-06 |title=Tories take Ockendon as Sue jumps sinking ship |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/ockendon-ward-result-tories-take-ockendon-131889 |access-date=2022-05-08 |website=Thurrock Nub News |language=en}}</ref> Meanwhile, Conservative council leader Rob Gledhill retained his seat with a small majority of 157, mirroring his near-defeat to [[UKIP]] in the [[2014 Thurrock Council election|2014 council elections]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Speight |first=Neil |date=2022-05-06 |title=Tory veteran councillor and leader battled hard for his victory |url=https://thurrock.nub.news/news/local-news/tory-veteran-councillor-and-leader-battled-hard-for-his-victory-131917 |access-date=2022-05-08 |website=Thurrock Nub News |language=en}}</ref>

The Conservatives won the popular vote with 12,442 votes, a small majority of 91 against Labour's 12,351. The election suffered a low turnout, with only one in four eligible voters participating.<ref name=":ElectionOverview"/>

